## Fix: Task 5 review round 1 (no _slots source, scheduled mode, continuous wait, view drop, sync trace)
================================================================================

Context: Review fix round 1 of Task 5 (Imp#2, Imp#3, Imp#4, Minor#1, trace
ruling).

Changes:

1. src/back/objects/digitaltwin/build/TwinLakebaseSync.py (Imp#2)
   `_slots` is never a Lakebase sync source in this release: `sync_plan_for`
   (no slots inputs any more) returns `_data` (table or MV) when it is a copy,
   else the gateway VIEW as a snapshot. Asserted-only readers (entity-search
   rebuild, `include_inferred=False` reads, BFS expansion) query `_sync`
   directly and would see one row per slot and blank objects.
   `slots_complete` stays computed; the slots union view / index DDL stays for
   a follow-up.
2. src/back/objects/registry/schedule/build.py,
   src/back/objects/digitaltwin/build/TwinBuildPostMaterialize.py (Imp#3)
   Scheduled builds follow the domain's materialization mode: the new
   `TwinBuildPostMaterialize.unprobed_mode_decision(domain, settings)` runs the
   resolver ladder with `decide_mode`'s inputs (shared `_resolver_inputs`:
   serverless flag, backend, previews={}; realtime -> microbatch), counting
   the sources as row-tracked because the scheduled path cannot probe them. A
   scheduled tick of an incremental domain keeps TRIGGERED, a full domain
   SNAPSHOT, instead of flipping the registration to SNAPSHOT each run.
3. src/back/core/graphdb/lakebase/ContinuousSyncWait.py (new),
   src/back/core/graphdb/lakebase/SyncedTableManager.py (Imp#4)
   `trigger_and_wait` on a CONTINUOUS table polls until the state leaves
   PROVISIONING* (ONLINE_CONTINUOUS_UPDATE or terminal OK), raises
   `InfrastructureError` on a terminal failure or after `timeout_s`, honours
   `cancel_check` and `on_state_change`; still never triggers an update.
4. src/back/core/graphdb/lakebase/SyncedTableManager.py,
   src/back/core/graphdb/lakebase/SyncedTableRegistration.py,
   src/back/objects/digitaltwin/build/TwinLakebaseSync.py (Minor#1)
   `ensure(..., before_delete=)` calls the hook right before a drifted
   registration is deleted; `TwinLakebaseSync.union_view_dropper` (best-effort)
   drops the Postgres union view there instead of before `ensure`, so a failed
   `ensure` never leaves readers without a view. Step 6 re-creates it.
5. src/back/objects/digitaltwin/build/TwinBuildPostMaterialize.py,
   src/back/objects/digitaltwin/build/_build_pipeline.py (trace ruling)
   New trace keys `sync_source` (the relation the synced table was registered
   on) and `sync_mode_effective` ("" when no sync ran), recorded by
   `TwinLakebaseSync.register` once `ensure` succeeded; `triples_source` keeps
   meaning the counted relation.
6. docs/backend.md, src/front/templates/settings.html,
   src/back/core/graphdb/lakebase/LakebaseBase.py
   Stored `triggered` / `continuous` values now act as caps on the
   mode-derived schedule (they were coerced to snapshot before); no text
   mentions `_slots` as a source any more; scheduled builds follow the
   domain's mode.
7. Tests: tests/units/core/test_synced_table_manager.py (continuous wait:
   streams, already online, failure, timeout, cancel; before_delete hook),
   tests/units/objects/test_build_pipeline_modes.py (plan never picks
   `_slots`; sync trace keys; view dropped only through the hook),
   tests/units/registry/test_scheduled_build_lakeflow_source.py (incremental
   tick -> triggered, microbatch/realtime -> continuous, full -> snapshot,
   cap, serverless off; unprobed_mode_decision).

Known limitations:
- The scheduled path counts the sources as row-tracked: for a domain whose
  sources are not tracked, the main build runs `full` (snapshot) while a
  scheduled tick registers the requested faster schedule, so those domains
  still recreate the synced table on each alternation.
- tests/units/objects/test_build_pipeline_compute_once.py still pins the old
  eight trace keys and the removed `_slots` sync warning (two failing tests);
  that file was reserved for a concurrent change in this round.

